  "meta": {
    "title": "Decimal numbers divisible by 3",
    "blurb": "The schoolroom rule — a number is divisible by 3 when its digit sum is — as a three-state machine. Ten symbols, one state per remainder, and every digit sends remainder r to (10r + d) mod 3 = (r + d) mod 3.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "0",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "12",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "369",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "1002",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "987654321",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "1",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "100",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "2024",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "divisibility",
        "remainder",
        "decimal"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "Reading a decimal number left to right, the value so far goes from *n* to 10*n* + *d*. Modulo 3, 10 ≡ 1, so the remainder goes from *r* to *r* + *d* — which is why adding up the digits works.\n\nDigits 0, 3, 6 and 9 never change the state; 1, 4 and 7 advance it by one; 2, 5 and 8 by two. The empty word is read as 0 and accepted. The same idea gives a *k*-state machine for divisibility by any *k* in any base: see **Binary divisibility by 3** and **by 5**."
    }
  }
}
